# (https://citation-file-format.github.io/).
#
# NOTE: CFF 1.2.0 has no native funding field. Funding sources (THRILL and
# further projects) are documented in the handbook's Funding section and in
# README.md, not here. See doc/book/src/concepts/introduction/funding.md.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  OPOSSUM — Open-source Optics Simulation Software and Unified Modeler
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file.
  OPOSSUM was started within the EU project THRILL (Technology for High-Repetition-rate Intense Laser Laboratories) and is further supported by
  BMFTR-funded project LASE-FUSE (LAser Simulation for Enhanced FUSion Efficiency); see the Funding section of the handbook for details.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Udo
    family-names: Eisenbarth
    email: u.eisenbarth@gsi.de
    affiliation: GSI Helmholtzzentrum für Schwerionenforschung GmbH, Plasma physics group / PHELIX
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0001-0168-5932'
  - given-names: Yannik
    family-names: Zobus
    email: y.zobus@gsi.de
    affiliation: GSI Helmholtzzentrum für Schwerionenforschung GmbH, Plasma physics group / LASE-FUSE
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0559-1167'
abstract: >-
  OPOSSUM is an open-source simulation platform for optical systems, with a
  special focus on designing and analyzing large-scale, high-energy/intensity
  laser systems. An optical setup is modeled as a graph whose nodes are optical
  components and whose edges are light connections between component ports; the
  graph can be processed by different analyzer modules (energy flow, ray
  tracing, ghost-focus analysis) that each produce a report.
keywords:
  - optics
  - laser
  - simulation
  - ray-tracing
  - optical-design
  - rust
repository-code: 'https://github.com/opossum-labs/opossum'
url: 'https://github.com/opossum-labs/opossum'
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
version: 0.7.3
date-released: '2026-08-14'
